        "section": "4.6 Radical-site initiation (alpha-cleavage)"
      },
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "curator_note": "Concrete example: the diethyl ether molecular ion, n-ionized at oxygen, CH3-CH2-O(+.)-C2H5 -> .CH3 + CH2=O(+)-C2H5 (spectrum in Figure 3.9). Book draws 3 arc(s); moves <1,2,3> are ALL drawn on the page -- the odd electron on O into the forming C=O bond, one electron of the CH3-CH2 sigma bond into that same bond, and the second electron of that sigma bond onto the departing methyl -- so no implicit hook is required. DELOCALISATION: the book adds the resonance partner (+)CH2-OC2H5 in parentheses; the oxocarbenium form CH2=O(+)-C2H5 is the one encoded here and the two are the same ion."
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence_mod_rules",
      "source_id": "mod_rules",
      "target_type": "mechanism",
      "target_id": "IMS4-EQ4.13",
      "support_type": "computationally_supported",
      "locator": {
        "page": 57,
        "equation": "4.13"
      },
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "curator_note": "Cross-linked MØD rules IMS_4_13_1 and IMS_4_13_2 (both atom-explicit for diethyl ether). VERIFIED by applying each rule to the O-ionized diethyl ether term graph in the container: IMS_4_13_1 gives C=[O+]CC + [CH3.], exactly the step encoded here (cleaves C1-C5, forms C5=O8, quenches the O radical); IMS_4_13_2 gives CCO[CH2+] + [CH3.], i.e. the resonance partner (+)CH2-OC2H5 the book prints in parentheses, emitted as a separate localized graph because MØD has no delocalisation. Both conserve atoms, charge and spin and survive the conservation guardrail."
    },
